# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading result sheets from photographs
#
# Three readers, and the keys decide which one runs: Anthropic first, then
# Google, then a reader built into the server that needs no key at all.
#
# ONLY THE FIRST CAN READ HANDWRITING, and the figures on an EC8A are written
# by hand. Tested against a real INEC form, the built-in reader recovered no
# vote figure at all — the only number it found on the page was the year in
# the title. If sheets are "not being read", this is almost always why: no
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set, so the built-in reader is doing the work.
#
# Nothing breaks without a key. A reading that cannot be trusted is shown to
# the agent and kept out of the form, and the figures are typed as they always
# were. Set this to get the feature the photograph is actually for.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# An optical reader, used when there is no Anthropic key. Reads handwriting
# well. Free for the first 1,000 sheets a month, but Google wants a card on
# file before it will issue the key.
GOOGLE_VISION_API_KEY=

# THE ONE YOU CAN TURN ON RIGHT NOW.
#
# OCR.space issues a free key against an email address — no card, no cloud
# account, no billing project — and its handwriting engine reads a real INEC
# form. Measured on one: twelve of fourteen handwritten figures recovered,
# where the built-in reader recovered none.
#
# It is the floor, not the ceiling. Nothing it reads fills an agent's form
# unless the arithmetic on the sheet corroborates it, so its misses show up as
# empty boxes to be typed rather than as wrong figures to be filed.
#
#   Get one at https://ocr.space/ocrapi  (free tier, 25,000 pages a month)
OCRSPACE_API_KEY=

# Override the choice above: claude | google | local | off.
# SHEET_READER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp (Meta Cloud API)
#
# All four, or none. Without them the channel still runs end to end: replies
# are recorded as QUEUED and shown on the desk, they simply never leave the
# building. That is what makes it demonstrable without an account.
#
# Where each one comes from, in the Meta developer console:
#
#   WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN  You invent it. Any long random string. Paste the
#                          same one into Meta's webhook setup box. It is only
#                          used for the one-time subscription handshake.
#   WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET    App settings → Basic → App secret (Show).
#                          Every delivery is signature checked against it.
#   WHATSAPP_TOKEN         WhatsApp → API Setup gives a temporary one good for
#                          24 hours, fine for a first test. For anything
#                          lasting, generate a system user token instead.
#   WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID      WhatsApp → API Setup, "Phone number ID". This is the
#                          long number under the phone number, not the phone
#                          number itself.
#
# Then point Meta's callback URL at:
#   https://your-domain/api/whatsapp/webhook
# and subscribe the WhatsApp account to the "messages" field. Subscribing is a
# separate click from verifying the URL, and nothing arrives until it is done.
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data Bank — where everything this product receives is also delivered
#
# Data Bank is the intake, classification and routing hub behind Poll360 and
# Agent360. Every registration, every photographed result sheet, every figure
# read off one, and everything that arrives over WhatsApp is sent there, where
# it is sealed and chained for the eighteen months between a polling day and a
# tribunal.
#
# Unset, nothing is sent and nothing breaks. The delivery is never on the path
# between an agent and a saved return — a hub having a bad night must not cost
# a polling unit its count — so an unconfigured hub is simply a quiet one.
#
# Whatever does not land is kept in `dumpsite_outbox` and can be sent again:
#
#   npm run databank:replay
#
DATABANK_URL=
DATABANK_API_KEY=

# The secret that signs every delivery, held by both products and sent nowhere.
#
# ── WHY A KEY WAS NOT ENOUGH ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The key above proves somebody knew the key. It does not prove that *this
# body* was the body that was sent, and it does not prove the request is
# recent — the key is identical on every request, so it says nothing about the
# one it is on. Anything positioned to alter a body in transit could change a
# figure and the key would still check out.
#
# That matters here more than almost anywhere, because what travels this
# pipeline is evidence: the photograph of Form EC8A before anybody read it,
# and the figures read off it, kept for the eighteen months between a polling
# day and a tribunal. Evidence that can be altered in transit is not evidence.
#
# The signature covers the exact bytes posted, carries the second it was made,
# and is refused by the hub if it is more than five minutes old.
#
# ── SET THE SAME VALUE ON BOTH PRODUCTS ─────────────────────────────────────
# Any long random string. Deliveries keep working while only one end has it —
# the signature is an extra header, which a receiver that has not been taught
# about it ignores — so it can be rolled out in either order, and does not
# need two deployments to land at the same instant.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
DATABANK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where a count that every instance must agree on is kept
#
# Failed sign-ins are counted here. Without it the count lives in one
# process's memory, which means "eight attempts" is eight attempts *per
# instance* — and on a platform that runs the application in as many instances
# as the traffic asks for, a caller working through a password list is handed
# a fresh budget every time they are routed somewhere new. On a quiet night
# there is one instance and the limiter works. On the night somebody is
# actually attacking it, there are forty, and it does not.
#
# Upstash's REST interface, because it needs no connection held open, which is
# the only shape that works on serverless hosting. Without it the count falls
# back to Postgres, which is correct and costs one round trip on the paths
# where somebody types a credential; without a database either, it falls back
# to memory and the health screen says so rather than hiding it.
#
# Nothing on a page-view path touches this.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How many proxies sit in front of this deployment
#
# ── THE SETTING THAT DECIDES WHETHER RATE LIMITING WORKS AT ALL ─────────────
# Every limit is counted against the caller's address, and the address has to
# be read out of a header — which the caller is also allowed to send. The only
# trustworthy entries are the ones our own infrastructure appended, and they
# are counted in from the *right*.
#
# One on Vercel, which is the platform's own edge. Two behind a load balancer
# of your own as well. Getting it wrong costs something in both directions:
#
#   too low   the address read is one of ours, so every caller in the world
#             shares one bucket and the first person to mistype their password
#             locks out everybody
#   too high  the address read is one the caller supplied, so anybody can have
#             a fresh budget on every request
#
# Leave it unset to take the default of 1.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a session is recognised without asking the database
#
# Who is asking is read from the database, which is what makes revoking a
# session or disabling an account take effect almost immediately. It is also
# one query per request from every signed-in person, which is the busiest
# query in the product: a hundred thousand people with a dashboard open is
# five thousand lookups a second before a figure has been counted.
#
# So the answer is held for a few seconds. What that costs, exactly:
#
#   signing out           unaffected — the browser no longer has the cookie
#   disabling an account  delayed by this many seconds
#   revoking a session    the same
#   changing a role       the same
#
# Five in production by default, and none in development so that testing
# "disable this account and watch them lose access" behaves as described.
# Set 0 to switch it off entirely and go back to asking every time.
#
# For a very large deployment, raising this to at least the dashboard refresh
# interval (30) is what actually reduces lookups — see SCALE.md §5.
SESSION_CACHE_SECONDS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The command dashboard's feed
#
# Command is the campaign's own screen: a named candidate, their photograph,
# and their share of a live count. It is the one screen in this product where
# a wrong figure is attributed to a real person by name.
#
# It is therefore off until a real transmission exists. With this unset the
# board is still built and every state is still drawn — there is simply
# nothing in it, and the screen says so above the zeroes rather than leaving a
# reader to wonder whether a quiet night is a broken page.
#
# Set it to `live` when returns are genuinely arriving. Nothing else changes:
# the screen is already reading from the database.
#
#   COMMAND_FEED=live
COMMAND_FEED=

